  "label": "TMUA early specimen · Paper 1 · Question 6",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA early specimen · Paper 1 · Question 6 — Sum parameter values making a linear factor divide a cubic.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E"
  ],
  "correct": "D",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Apply the factor theorem",
      "body": "<p>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> means that substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> must give zero. This will be an equation in c.</p>",
      "recap": "A polynomial factor condition can determine a parameter.",
      "pitfall": "The factor supplies a value of x, not a value of c.",
      "pause": "Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l02",
          "reminder": "A polynomial factor condition can determine a parameter."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Collect the parameter equation",
      "body": "Expand and simplify after substitution, then identify the resulting quadratic in the parameter. Since only the sum is requested, compare coefficients or solve both roots.",
      "recap": "The sum of a quadratic’s roots is the negative linear coefficient divided by its leading coefficient.",
      "pitfall": "Account for the multiplier outside the squared bracket before collecting like powers.",
      "pause": "Derive the parameter quadratic and find the sum of all its permitted roots.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l02",
          "reminder": "The sum of a quadratic’s roots is the negative linear coefficient divided by its leading coefficient."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>By the factor theorem, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Expanding gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Divide by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>; both satisfy the original factor condition.</p><p>The requested sum is <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, option D.</strong> It can also be read directly as the negative of the linear coefficient of the monic quadratic.</p>",
